On the machine-assisted translation
-----------------------------------

That the MATLAB-to-Python translation was performed with an AI assistant does
not put the result outside copyright. Two points bear on it:

  - The copyright in the MATLAB original governs its translations. A
    translation is a derivative work whether a person or a tool performs it,
    so permission from the MATLAB authors is needed regardless. If anything, a
    machine translation of specific source is further from an independent
    reimplementation than a human rewrite would be, not closer to one.

  - Under current U.S. Copyright Office guidance, material generated by a
    machine without human authorship is not itself protectable, but human
    selection, arrangement, correction, and subsequent modification are. The
    site-specific work, the debugging recorded in BUGFIXES.txt, and the
    continued development are human authorship on any reading.

The practical effect is that this does not simplify the licensing question,
and all three parties above should be part of settling it. This is a
description of the situation, not legal advice.


